Subject: [PATCH 1/5] dma: one-shot (Prog mode) descriptor arming

Prog mode is already in the gateware: LitePCIeDMAScatterGather documents it and loop_prog_n = 0
selects it. Only the driver lacked a way to use it. This adds the ioctls to arm N descriptors for a
single transfer, with a short final descriptor so a batch need not be a whole number of buffers.

The hw_count arithmetic needs its own branch. In Loop mode the table wraps and count means 'times
wrapped'; in Prog mode index counts descriptors monotonically and the register IS the count. Reading
it the loop-mode way scales by DMA_BUFFER_COUNT instead of 65536 and yields a plausible wrong answer.

Arming disables the engine, programs the table and enables it again, which is the same shape as
litepcie_dma_writer_start and for the same reason. The descriptor splitter latches the head of the
table into its own registers and is only held in reset while the engine is disabled, so flushing the
table alone leaves a descriptor behind. See the comment on litepcie_dma_arm.

The default is unchanged: oneshot lives in the devm_kzalloc'd device struct, so every loop-mode path
is byte for byte what it was.

Closing the file that enabled one-shot puts the channel back in loop mode. Only that file does, so a
process that opens the node and closes it again, which device discovery does, leaves the mode of the
application using the channel alone.

--- a/litepcie.h
+++ b/litepcie.h
@@ -72,6 +72,29 @@
 	int64_t sw_count;
 };
 
+/* One-shot (Prog mode) DMA.
+ *
+ * The descriptor table has always had two modes. In Loop mode each descriptor is refilled to the table
+ * as it is consumed, so the DMA cycles the buffer ring forever. In Prog mode a descriptor is executed
+ * once and the DMA stops when the table is empty. The driver has only ever used Loop mode, so Prog mode
+ * is reachable from gateware but not from software.
+ *
+ * Loop mode is right for a continuous stream. It is wrong for request/response traffic, where the device
+ * re-reads stale buffers between batches and the host cannot tell a fresh result from an old one without
+ * putting a sequence number in the data. Prog mode removes that: the device sees exactly what was armed,
+ * so position alone identifies a transfer.
+ *
+ * Set the mode before starting the DMA. Then arm a batch of descriptors for each transfer.
+ */
+struct litepcie_ioctl_dma_oneshot {
+	uint8_t enable;      /* 0: loop mode (default, unchanged behaviour), 1: one-shot */
+};
+
+struct litepcie_ioctl_dma_arm {
+	uint32_t count;       /* buffers to arm, 1..DMA_BUFFER_COUNT */
+	uint32_t last_length; /* bytes in the final buffer, 0 for a full DMA_BUFFER_SIZE */
+};
+
